<p class="meta">2026-10-17 · updated 2026-10-21 · money · manufacturing</p>
<h1>A corrugator engineer values International Paper</h1>
<p class="lede">I've stood next to the machine that makes this company's product for eleven years. I'd never valued the company. The stock is down {{chg_ytd_words}} this year, so I did it properly — Wall Street Prep's mechanics, Damodaran's discipline, and the one thing neither of them has: what the numbers look like from the floor.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>Updated October 21.</strong> I went back and pulled IP's FY2025 10-K for one reason: pensions and leases are where a paper company keeps the debt that "net debt" leaves out. The pensions and the leases came back clean. A footnote didn't: a {{tk_dtl}} tax bill from a 2007 timberland sale, due in 2027. Neither did the reserves for old mill sites and asbestos claims, {{tk_env_asb}} more. All in, the 10-K takes {{ps_total_abs}} a share off the DCF ({{was_dcf_ps2}} → {{dcf_ps2}}) and moves the down-cycle odds the market is pricing from {{was_market_odds}} to {{market_odds}}. Every number below has been rerun from the model; <a href="#debt">the line-by-line is here</a>.</div>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>price</b><em>{{price}}</em><i>{{price_date}} · 52-wk {{wk52}}</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>YTD</b><em>{{chg_ytd}}</em><i>{{chg_12m}} over 12 months</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>yield</b><em>{{yield}}</em><i>{{dps}}/share · ~{{div_cost}}/yr</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>net debt</b><em>{{net_debt_b}}</em><i>EV ≈ {{ev_mkt_b}}</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>debt-like, 10-K</b><em>{{debt_like}}</em><i>after tax · <span style="white-space:nowrap">{{ps_total}}/share</span></i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>2026 EBITDA guide</b><em>{{guide}}</em><i>was {{guide_jan}} in January</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>2025 free cash flow</b><em>{{fcf25}}</em><i>the dividend wasn't covered</i></div>
</div>

<div class="sect">the story first</div>
<p>Damodaran's rule is that every valuation is a story with numbers attached, and the story comes first, because the numbers are only as good as the story they're consistent with. Here's the story as I see it from the plant, before I open a spreadsheet.</p>

<div class="story">
  <div><b>the bull case: capacity out, price in</b>
  <ul>
    <li>IP closed Savannah (1.0 million tons a year) and Riceboro (430k) in September 2025, after Red River and Orange. The industry took out <strong>3.9 million tons in 2025 — nearly 10% of North American containerboard capacity</strong>.</li>
    <li>Three price increases in 2026. The index has recognized about <strong>{{idx}} a ton on linerboard</strong>. The CFO's own sensitivity: <strong>every $10 a ton is {{sens10}} of EBITDA</strong> a year.</li>
    <li>80/20 found ~$510M of run-rate cost in 2025 and expects the same in 2026. Thirty-one EMEA closures, 2,800 positions.</li>
    <li>They bought the NORPAC mill in Washington for $360M because, in the CEO's words, they were "significantly short paper on the West Coast and shipping it there uneconomically." Anyone who's watched a truck of linerboard cross three states knows what that costs.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div><b>the bear case: expensive assets, soft demand</b>
  <ul>
    <li>Demand is flat. The trade forecast for 2026 containerboard went from +2% to <strong>−2%</strong> after February. The bank analysts are "mostly worried about demand."</li>
    <li>EMEA (the DS Smith business) lost $80M in Q2 because paper prices rose faster than box prices. That lag is real; I've lived it from the other side.</li>
    <li>The 10% capacity cut was cost economics — low performers out — not a demand squeeze. Those mills aren't coming back, which supports price, but the tightness is thinner than the headline.</li>
    <li>Paper is capital-hungry. D&A is {{da_pct}} of sales; capex is running {{capex26_b}} a year. The dividend costs {{div_cost}} and 2025 free cash flow was negative. The {{yield}} yield is the market telling you it doesn't believe the dividend is safe.</li>
  </ul></div>
</div>

<p>And one thing that isn't in either column because it happened this week: IP announced in January it would spin off EMEA, and on October 15 it said it had unsolicited bids and was running a sale process in parallel. The DS Smith capital markets day is November 3. So the question "what is the sum of the parts worth" is no longer academic.</p>

<div class="sect">narrative to numbers</div>
<p>A DCF is a machine that turns operating assumptions into a price. The machine is standard; the assumptions are everything. So here is 2027 built as a bridge from 2026 guidance, with each piece tied to the story, and with my own judgments marked as judgments.</p>

<div class="viz" id="bridge"><div class="cap">The 2027 bridge for the North American business, $ millions. Grey is company guidance; orange are my judgments (the model's yellow cells); green is what falls out — and the deliberate step down to mid-cycle.</div></div>

<p>The judgments, stated plainly so you can disagree with them:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>{{stick_Word}} the price sticks.</strong> Of the {{idx}} a ton the index has recognized, I assume {{stick}} holds at the box level through 2027. Index-linked contracts lag by months; the third round hasn't reached the open market yet; a soft year gives some of it back. {{stick_Word}}, net of the {{in_guide}} already in 2026 guidance, is about {{price_bridge}} of 2027 EBITDA.</li>
  <li><strong>Volume up {{vol}}.</strong> The market is flat-to-down; IP says it's outperforming by three or four points. I split the difference and assume a {{contrib}} contribution margin on the increment, which is what a box plant running through existing mills earns on the next ton.</li>
  <li><strong>{{costout}} of cost-out against {{infl}} of inflation.</strong> OCC is back to $80–100 a ton from the 2025 lows, freight is up, and 80/20 keeps finding money. Net of each other, it's roughly a wash — which is what a transformation looks like from the P&L two years in.</li>
  <li><strong>Then it comes back down.</strong> This is the Damodaran part people skip. 2027 is a price-increase year, and a price-increase year is a <em>peak</em>. A cyclical gets valued at mid-cycle, not at the peak. So 2028 onward I use a mid-cycle NA margin of {{na_margin}} — between Smurfit Westrock's {{sw_margin}} and Packaging Corp's {{pkg_margin}} — and the model deliberately shows EBITDA stepping <em>down</em> from 2027 to 2028. If that looks wrong to you, that's the assumption to argue with.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sect" id="debt">where the debt hides</div>
<p>Net debt is what IP owes its lenders less its cash: {{net_debt_b}} at the end of June. A paper company owes more than that. It owes pensions to the people who ran its mills, health care it promised them, rent on its railcars and warehouses, the cleanup of mills it no longer runs, and taxes it put off. None of it is in net debt, and all of it gets paid before a shareholder sees a dollar. That's why I went back and pulled the FY2025 10-K. Line by line:</p>
<div style="overflow-x:auto"><table class="sens xl txt">
  <tr><th>item</th><th>what the 10-K says (Dec 31, 2025)</th><th>how it's counted</th><th>per share</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Pension plans in surplus</td><td>{{tk_surplus}}. The U.S. qualified plan holds {{tk_us_q_assets}} against {{tk_us_q_pbo}} owed; the UK plan is {{tk_uk_surplus}} over.</td><td>{{tk_surplus_counted}}: a surplus is hard to get back out (counting it adds {{ps_surplus_if}})</td><td class="n">{{ps_surplus}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pension plans in deficit</td><td>{{tk_deficit}}, including {{tk_us_nq}} of U.S. nonqualified plans with no assets behind them</td><td>after tax</td><td class="n">{{ps_deficit}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Retiree medical and postemployment</td><td>{{tk_opeb}}</td><td>after tax</td><td class="n">{{ps_opeb}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Environmental reserves</td><td>{{tk_env}}, the "estimated probable liability" for old mill sites</td><td>after tax</td><td class="n">{{ps_env}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Asbestos claims</td><td>{{tk_asb}}, net of insurance</td><td>after tax</td><td class="n">{{ps_asb}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Brazil tax guarantee to Sylvamo</td><td>{{tk_brazil_booked}} booked, of a potential {{tk_brazil}}</td><td>the booked part, in full</td><td class="n">{{ps_brazil_booked}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Operating leases</td><td>{{tk_op_leases}}</td><td>not added: the rent is already inside EBITDA</td><td class="n">{{ps_zero}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Finance leases</td><td>{{tk_fin_leases}}</td><td>already inside the debt</td><td class="n">{{ps_zero}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tax on the Temple-Inland timber notes</td><td>{{tk_dtl}}, "settled with the maturity of the notes in 2027"</td><td>in full</td><td class="n">{{ps_dtl}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The timber notes, net of their loans</td><td>{{tk_vie_assets}} of notes against {{tk_vie_liab}} of nonrecourse loans</td><td>what's left over is IP's</td><td class="n">{{ps_vie}}</td></tr>
  <tr><td style="font-weight:600">What the 10-K takes off the value</td><td>{{debt_like}} after tax</td><td></td><td class="n" style="font-weight:600">{{ps_total}}</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p class="src">After tax at the model's {{tax}} rate, per share on {{shares}} shares. Pension and lease balances are at December 31, 2025, the last time IP measured them; debt and cash are from June 30, 2026. Every line is an input in the workbook with its quote from the filing, and two of the {{n_checks_word}} checks tie them back to the 10-K's own totals.</p>
<p>The pensions came back clean. The U.S. plan holds {{tk_us_q_assets}} against {{tk_us_q_pbo}} owed, and the 10-K says IP's funded plans are "fully funded on a projected benefit obligation basis." I count the plans in deficit and give no credit for the plans in surplus. The leases came back clean in a different way: under U.S. GAAP the cost of an operating lease is an operating expense, so the rent is already inside the EBITDA the whole model runs on. Adding the {{tk_op_leases}} liability as debt too would count the same rent twice.</p>
<p>The reserves are what a hundred-year-old paper company carries for a long time: {{tk_env}} for cleaning up mill sites it no longer runs (Cass Lake, the Kalamazoo River, San Jacinto, Versailles Pond) and {{tk_asb}} for asbestos claims after insurance. They're booked, the cash goes out over years, and adjusted EBITDA leaves them out, so they count: {{ps_env}} and {{ps_asb}} a share.</p>
<p>The footnote is the part I didn't expect. In 2007 Temple-Inland sold its timberland for notes instead of cash and put off the tax; IP inherited both when it bought Temple-Inland in 2012. The 10-K says it plainly: "{{tk_dtl}} deferred tax liability related to the Temple-Inland timber monetization, which will be settled with the maturity of the notes in 2027." That year the notes ({{tk_vie_assets}}) pay off the loans taken against them ({{tk_vie_liab}}) and leave {{tk_vie_equity}}, and the tax takes {{tk_dtl}}. Net, {{ps_timber}} a share, due the same year {{tk_debt_2027}} of IP's regular debt matures.</p>
<p>Two things I didn't count, so you can: about {{tk_utb}} of unrecognized tax benefits, which IP can't say when or whether it will pay ({{ps_utb}} a share if all of it were), and the rest of a Brazilian tax assessment IP agreed to share with Sylvamo, the printing-papers business it spun off. IP puts its potential share at about {{tk_brazil}}, has booked {{tk_brazil_booked}} of it (counted above), and a first-round court ruling went its way; the other {{tk_brazil_rest}} would be {{ps_brazil_rest}} a share.</p>
<div class="pull">All in, the 10-K takes {{ps_total_abs}} a share off the DCF, {{was_dcf_ps2}} to {{dcf_ps2}}, and moves the down-cycle odds the market is pricing from {{was_market_odds}} to {{market_odds}}. My edge narrows from {{was_edge}} to {{edge}}. What the bet is on doesn't change: the discount isn't hidden debt. It's the cycle.</div>

<div class="sect">what the machine says</div>
<p>Three independent routes, same inputs, and they land in the same place, which is the first thing worth knowing.</p>
<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>DCF, mid-cycle</b><em>{{dcf_ps}}</em><i>WACC {{wacc}} · TV is {{tv_share}} of EV</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>sum of the parts</b><em>{{sotp_ps}}</em><i>NA at {{sotp_na_mult}} · DS Smith at {{sotp_emea_mult}}</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>bear / mid / bull</b><em>{{bear_ps}} / {{mid_ps}} / {{bull_ps}}</em><i>weighted {{weights}}: {{pw_ps}}</i></div>
</div>
<p>Two honesty checks the model prints on purpose. The terminal value is {{tv_share}} of the enterprise value — normal for a mature company, and it means the answer <em>is</em> the terminal assumptions, which is why the next section exists. And the Gordon-growth terminal value implies an exit multiple of {{tv_mult}}, right between Smurfit Westrock and Packaging Corp, so the growth-and-discount-rate pair isn't quietly telling a story the comps don't.</p>

<p>The two assumptions that decide the number, with the market price for scale:</p>
{{table:grid}}
<p class="src">Grey cells are at or below the {{price}} market price. Read it as a map of what you'd have to believe.</p>

<div class="pull">At {{price}}, the market is paying a Smurfit Westrock multiple for the North American business and giving DS Smith almost nothing.</div>

<p>That's the thing the model made me see. Back out what the price implies and the stock trades at {{mkt_ev_26}} 2026 guidance and about {{mkt_ev_27}} the 2027 bridge. The market isn't disagreeing with the arithmetic; it's disagreeing with the story. It assumes 80/20 doesn't earn IP a multiple above SW's, that most of the {{idx}} doesn't stick, and that the dividend gets cut. Those are three versions of one belief: that the cycle turns down before the transformation shows up.</p>

<div class="sect">the bet, priced</div>
<p>A point estimate of {{dcf_ps}} is not a bet; it's a sentence. A bet needs a distribution. So I ran the same chain {{n_runs_words}} times with the decisive assumptions drawn from ranges instead of set to values — how much price sticks, the mid-cycle margin, the exit multiple, the discount rate, volume, capex intensity — plus a {{noise}} noise term on the terminal value for the possibility that the model's structure is simply wrong.</p>
<p>The first version said 90% of outcomes were above {{price}}. That was a lie, and a common one: I'd drawn the assumptions independently, and in a downturn they move together — price doesn't stick, demand falls, margins compress and the multiple shrinks all at once. So the second version draws the <em>cycle</em> first, with a {{p_down}} chance of a down-cycle over the horizon, and draws everything else conditional on it.</p>

<div class="viz" id="dist"><div class="cap">{{n_runs_Words}} values per share. The dashed line is the {{price}} price; the tails are what the point estimate hides.</div></div>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>P(worth more than {{price}})</b><em>{{p_above}}</em><i>given a down-cycle: {{p_above_down}} · given not: {{p_above_base}}</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>median / mean</b><em>{{median}} / {{mean}}</em><i>10th pct {{p10}} · 90th pct {{p90}}</i></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>P(worth less than {{half_price}})</b><em>{{p_half}}</em><i>P(worth more than {{double_price}}): {{p_double}}</i></div>
</div>

<p>Then the question that matters: what does the probability of a down-cycle have to be for the bet to be fair?</p>

<div class="viz" id="cycle"><div class="cap">The whole bet is one number. P(value &gt; {{price}}) as the probability of a containerboard down-cycle over the next three years goes from {{sweep_lo}} to {{sweep_hi}}.</div></div>

<div class="pull">At {{price}} the market is pricing a coin flip on a down-cycle. If you think it's {{p_down}}, the shares are worth about {{median_at_me}} and you have an edge. If you think it's {{market_odds}}, you're being paid nothing.</div>

<p>Everything else — the DCF, the comps, the sum of the parts — collapses into that one judgment. Which is the useful thing about doing this properly: it tells you what you're actually betting on, and it isn't the dividend or the spin-off or the price increase. It's the cycle. And the cycle is the thing you can watch: box shipments, the index in October and November, OCC, and what the DS Smith buyers are willing to pay on November 3.</p>

<div class="sect">price your own bet</div>
<p>Everything above is my guess. Here is the same machine with the guesses exposed. Set what <em>you</em> believe — how much of the {{idx}} sticks, the mid-cycle margin, what a buyer pays in 2030, the discount rate, and the odds of a down-cycle — and the page prices the bet the way the spreadsheet and the Monte Carlo do, live.</p>
<div class="bet" id="bet">
  <div class="knobs">
    <label>price that sticks<input type="range" id="bStick" min="0" max="100" value="{{stick_pts}}"><span class="v" id="vStick">{{stick}} of {{idx}}/t</span></label>
    <label>NA mid-cycle margin<input type="range" id="bMargin" min="12" max="21" step="0.5" value="{{na_margin_pts}}"><span class="v" id="vMargin">{{na_margin_1}}</span></label>
    <label>exit multiple (2030)<input type="range" id="bMult" min="6" max="12" step="0.25" value="{{exit_mult_val}}"><span class="v" id="vMult">{{exit_mult}}</span></label>
    <label>WACC<input type="range" id="bWacc" min="6" max="9" step="0.1" value="{{wacc_pts}}"><span class="v" id="vWacc">{{wacc}}</span></label>
    <label>P(down-cycle, 3 yrs)<input type="range" id="bDown" min="5" max="80" value="{{p_down_pts}}"><span class="v" id="vDown">{{p_down}}</span></label>
  </div>
  <div class="out">
    <div><b>your value / share</b><em id="oVal">—</em></div>
    <div><b>vs {{price}}</b><em id="oUp">—</em></div>
    <div><b>P(value &gt; {{price}})</b><em id="oP">—</em></div>
    <div><b>10th / 90th pct</b><em id="oPct">—</em></div>
    <div><b>2027 EBITDA</b><em id="oE">—</em></div>
  </div>
  <svg id="bHist" viewBox="0 0 680 120" role="img" aria-label="Distribution of value under your beliefs"></svg>
  <p class="verdict" id="oVerdict"></p>
</div>

<p>On sizing: Kelly on this distribution says a half-Kelly position of about {{half_kelly_about}} of a portfolio, which is absurd, and the absurdity is the lesson. Kelly assumes you know the distribution. The distribution is the thing you're least sure of — I assigned that {{p_down}} myself. Treat Kelly as an upper bound, then divide by the number of things you could be wrong about. For one stock in one cyclical industry, that's a small position or none.</p>

<div class="sect">would I</div>
<p>Here's where I've landed, as a person and not as advice. The honest version of this stock is a bet on the containerboard cycle, with a {{yield}} yield that may or may not survive, a separation that may or may not unlock value, and a management that has been doing the hard, expensive, unpopular work of closing the mills everyone knew should close. If I want the quality, I'd own Packaging Corp at {{pkg_mult0}} and sleep. If I want the same multiple with less drama, Smurfit Westrock is right there at {{sw_mult0}}. IP is the one where my information is worth something — because the cycle call is the whole bet, and the cycle is what I read every week in box plants.</p>
<p>That's an argument for a small position and a watch list, not for conviction. The model is the thing I'm confident in. The {{p_down}} is the thing I'm not.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>What to watch, in order of how fast it would change my mind</strong> (kept current in the <a href="ip-thesis-log.html">thesis log</a>): Fibre Box Association shipments for Q4 (demand); the Fastmarkets index in October and November (does the third round hold); the Q3 call's price realization number against the {{in_guide}} that was in guidance; the November 3 DS Smith day and any sale price (the SOTP); OCC above $120 (the cost side). Each one is a row in the model.</div>

<div class="sect">what drives it</div>
<p>Before sizing anything, which assumption actually moves the answer? Rank correlation between each simulated input and the value per share, across the twenty thousand runs. The cycle dominates: the mid-cycle margin and the exit multiple top the list, and both are drawn from the cycle regime, which ranks third on its own. The pricing guess matters, but less than the cycle it sits inside.</p>
<div class="viz" id="tornado"><div class="cap">Spearman rank correlation of each input with value per share. Negative means a higher input lowers the value.</div></div>

<div class="sect">my bet, as instruments</div>
<p>The model values the business; a position pays on the <em>price</em>, and the two can disagree for a long time. So this adds one more assumption — over about a year the price closes {{convergence}} of the gap toward the simulated value, with {{mkt_noise}} of market noise on top — and maps that onto the four ways I could actually express the view, per 100 shares. Option premiums are placeholders from a reasonable chain; check the live one before doing anything.</p>
<div class="viz" id="payoff"><div class="cap">Payoff at the horizon for each instrument (lines) over the distribution of the horizon price (bars). The dashed line is today's {{price}}.</div></div>
{{table:instruments}}
<p>The table is the "win rate is a proxy" lesson on my own bet. The cash-secured put wins <strong>{{put_win}} of the time</strong> and has the <em>lowest</em> expected return on capital, because the {{put_lose}} is where it loses {{put_worst_round}} against {{put_premium}} of premium. The call spread has the best return on capital and loses money {{spread_lose}} of the time; in {{spread_wipe}} of runs it loses the whole {{spread_cost}}. Shares are the boring middle: {{shares_odds}} odds, {{shares_roc}} expected, and they pay the dividend while I wait, if it survives. None of this is advice; it's the distribution, priced four ways.</p>
<details class="code"><summary>show the code (bet.py, as run)</summary><pre>{{include:bet.py}}</pre></details>

<div class="sect">does it tie out</div>
<p>A model that evaluates is not a model that's right. So the numbers are checked the way a reviewer would check them — every identity, every cross-check between methods, every claim against what the company itself said — and the checks run as a script that fails loudly. {{n_checks_Word}} checks; all pass. The ones that matter most: the 2026 cash flow lands inside the company's own guide, the Gordon terminal value implies an exit multiple the comps support, the Python reproduces the spreadsheet to the dollar, the Monte Carlo's breakeven is where the arithmetic says it should be, and the 10-K's pension lines tie to the filing's own totals.</p>
<details class="code" open><summary>validation output</summary><pre>{{include:validate_out.txt}}</pre></details>
<details class="code"><summary>show the checks (validate.py)</summary><pre>{{include:validate.py}}</pre></details>
<p>What a green run proves and what it doesn't: the arithmetic is consistent and tied to its sources. It cannot prove the {{p_down}}, the {{na_margin}}, or the {{exit_mult0}}. Those are the judgment, and the log is where they get tested against time.</p>

<div class="sect">the workbook, rendered</div>
<p>The same spreadsheet, so you don't have to open it. Blue is a hardcoded input, yellow is a judgment of mine, everything else is a formula. The provenance column is the point: every number says where it came from.</p>
