<p class="src" style="margin-top:10px">The browser panel above uses the same chain with a 3,000-draw simulation, so its percentages differ from the Python run by a point or two.</p>

<div class="sect">the model and the code</div>
<p>The spreadsheet is <a href="../models/ip-valuation/IP_valuation_model.xlsx">IP_valuation_model.xlsx</a>: {{n_formulas}} live formulas, a Dictionary sheet that explains every term in plant language, every input tagged with its source (the FY2025 10-K, the Q2 2026 release, the Q1 and Q4 calls, Fastmarkets via Packaging Dive, Multiples.vc), and every judgment of mine in yellow so you can overwrite it. The Monte Carlo is <a href="../models/ip-valuation/montecarlo.py">montecarlo.py</a>, which mirrors the sheet's scenario chain line for line; <a href="../models/ip-valuation/build_model.py">build_model.py</a> generates the workbook, <a href="../models/ip-valuation/validate.py">validate.py</a> runs the {{n_checks_word}} tie-out checks, and <a href="../models/ip-valuation/bet.py">bet.py</a> maps the distribution onto the instruments. Public information only. Not investment advice; I'm an engineer who builds solvers, not a financial advisor, and I may hold or trade any of these names.</p>
<p>The mechanics are the ones Wall Street Prep teaches — unlevered free cash flow, WACC, a terminal value two ways, comps and sum-of-the-parts. The discipline is Damodaran's: story first, mid-cycle for a cyclical, show what the terminal value carries, and treat the answer as a distribution because that's what it is. The floor is mine.</p>

<p class="src">Written by Jason Fetzer, edited with Claude. Sources: IP Form 10-K for 2025 (filed February 2026); IP Q2 2026 earnings release; Packaging Dive on the Q1 2026 call (Apr 30), the Savannah/Riceboro closures (Aug 2025) and Fastmarkets RISI pricing (Sept 18, 2026); MarketBeat Q4 2025 call summary (Jan 29, 2026); Investing.com on the EMEA sale process (Oct 15, 2026); Multiples.vc for PKG and SW; Benzinga quote (Oct 15, 2026).</p>

